<Plain
	title="About · today.duinlab.nl"
	description="What today.duinlab.nl is, what it keeps, where its facts come from, and its code."
>
	<Fragment slot="title">About this page</Fragment>

	<p>
		A daily page for the two of us in The Hague, open to anyone: the weather, a few pictures, a few facts, a
		word, a quiz, a handful of games, new recipes, and what's on nearby. It changes at midnight, Amsterdam time.
	</p>

	<h2>Privacy</h2>
	<p>
		No cookies, no analytics. Your played games, streaks, quiz answers stay in this browser's local storage
		(<code>today.games.v1</code>, <code>today.quiz.v1</code>,
		and <code>today.sunrise.v1</code> for the date of your last visit) and are never sent anywhere. Clear the site's data
		to remove them.
	</p>
	<p>
		A Cloudflare Worker serves the page. Cloudflare keeps request logs for a few days: the address asked for,
		the time, and connection details such as your IP address. I only read them to trace a fault. The day's pictures load straight from NASA, The Met, Wikimedia, the Art Institute, the Rijksmuseum or YouTube, so
		they see your IP address too.
	</p>

	<h2>Accessibility</h2>
	<p>
		The aim is WCAG 2.2 AA. Text contrast holds in every sky, games and answers are at least 44 pixels tall,
		keyboard focus is visible, motion stops when your system asks for less, and the page reads in full without
		JavaScript. CI scans the home, about and 404 pages with axe. If something gets in your way,
		<a href="https://github.com/simonvanlierde/duinlab-today/issues">open an issue</a>.
	</p>

	<h2>Sources</h2>
	<p>Each item on the page links to where it came from.</p>
	<ul>
		<li>Facts, the featured article and on this day: Wikipedia. The word: Wiktionary.</li>
		<li>
			The pictures: NASA APOD, The Metropolitan Museum of Art, Wikipedia's picture of the day, the Art
			Institute of Chicago and the Rijksmuseum.
		</li>
		<li>The quiz: Open Trivia DB (CC BY-SA 4.0). The quote: ZenQuotes.</li>
		<li>
			The weather: <a href="https://open-meteo.com/">Open-Meteo</a>. The rain line comes from
			<a href="https://www.buienradar.nl/">Buienradar</a>'s radar, or from Open-Meteo when Buienradar is down.
			The weather line opens the week ahead on
			<a href="https://www.meteoblue.com/en/weather/week/the-hague_netherlands_2747373">meteoblue</a>.
		</li>
		<li>
			Air quality and pollen: Open-Meteo, shown only when the European AQI is 60 or more or a pollen count
			is high by MeteoSwiss's classes.
		</li>
		<li>
			Tide times at Scheveningen: <a href="https://waterinfo.rws.nl/">Rijkswaterstaat</a>. The sea temperature
			and waves: <a href="https://open-meteo.com/en/docs/marine-weather-api">Open-Meteo's marine API</a>.
		</li>
		<li>
			This week's shows and events: <a href="https://www.paard.nl/">Paard</a>,
			<a href="https://www.amare.nl/">Amare</a> and <a href="https://denhaag.com/en/agenda">DenHaag.com</a>.
		</li>
		<li>Recipes: the newest from the Guardian, BBC Good Food and Love and Lemons.</li>
		<li>
			The planets, full moons, solstices, equinoxes and eclipses:
			<a href="https://github.com/cosinekitty/astronomy">Astronomy Engine</a> (MIT). The meteor shower peaks
			are fixed approximate dates.
		</li>
	</ul>
	<p>Your browser works out the sky from the sun's position over The Hague.</p>

	<h2>Code</h2>
	<p>
		<a href="https://github.com/simonvanlierde/duinlab-today">github.com/simonvanlierde/duinlab-today</a>, MIT
		licence. The day's data is at <a href="/today.json">today.json</a>.
	</p>
</Plain>
